{
	title: 'Messages carry their sender; the send tool',
	spec: "A message says who sent it: the user, or another session. The host sets the sender from the sending session, never from what a client claims. The model sees a session's message prefixed with who sent it (tab, id and name, like the old Hal's '[Inbox · <id>]' line) and every client shows the sender on the message, so nobody mistakes an agent for the user. The user's messages steer by default (task 2r). A `send` tool (one file under src/host/tools/) lets a session message another by tab number or id: advisory by default, `steer: true` to steer as the user would, `queue: true` to run it after the target's current turn. Its required `description` is one plain-language sentence for the user: the sender's tool card is headed by it, and the message carries it as `summary`, so the recipient's clients show a folded card headed by the summary (web) or the summary over a three-line glimpse (terminal), the raw text one tap away. A subagent's report carries its `<summary>` line the same way. Models write inter-agent text tersely, sometimes without spaces; the user reads the summary, not that text. An advisory message is delivered as soon as possible, at the same point as steering (with the next tool results, or right after a round that had none), framed so the recipient reads it now but needn't drop its work for it; an idle recipient gets it as a turn of its own and gives it full attention. Sending to itself or to an unknown session is an error result. Up never recalls a message from another session (task dg). Artifacts: old-send.ts (the old tool), old-api-messages-excerpt.ts (how the old Hal framed inbox messages).",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"Sender = { from?, label?, advisory? } (common/blocks.ts) sits on inbox records, InboxItem and each text block of a user record (UserText); `label` ('tab 2 · 156-eds · name') is fixed when sent, so replay stays byte-identical for the prompt cache. Events: turn-start.sender, prompt.senders (parallel to texts, only when some text is another session's); transcript prompt Items carry from and label, and both clients show '(sent from <label>)' like commands.",
		"replay.framed puts '[Inbox · <label>]' above another session's text and, if advisory, replay.advisoryNote below it. Advisory is dropped whenever the message runs as a turn of its own (idle submit, drain, queue), so it only frames mid-turn deliveries.",
		"The protocol's submit no longer has `from`: only the host (send tool calling prompts.submit with a Sender) marks a sender; a client's `from` is ignored. Tests that need another session's message call prompts.submit directly.",
		"Decision: a message from another session to a paused, failed or blocked session waits in the inbox (like a queued one) instead of starting a turn, so an agent never undoes the user's pause; continue delivers it.",
		"ToolContext now has sessionId (the calling session). prompts.amend edits the human's last text in the last prompt, keeping others' texts and their senders; a prompt with no human text makes the edit go on top.",
		"Sender.summary rides the same paths as label (inbox.sender copies it). It is display-only: replay.framed ignores it, so the model's prompt is unchanged. GPT-6.1 Sol sent messages like 'Parenttracedpy65 PSIDWizballsubtune4…'; the summary keeps that noise folded.",
		'Bug fix: an opened report card showed its raw <summary> tag at the end of the body although the summary already heads the card. Invariant restored: a message carrying a summary displays its text without the summary tag (web body and terminal glimpse, via summary.strip); the model still receives the full text.'
	],
	needs: ['gr', 'dg', '2r']
